<p>
After the first attempt at making a very side-effectful circuit simulator, and
then an ambitious attempt at making it pure, I was reminded of a middle ground,
using a pattern common in production Haskell code. It is called <i>MTL-style
effects</i>, and it is a specific type of <i>final tagless encoding</i>.
</p>



<p>
The idea is that we first find a set of operations that our circuit simulator
needs to support. Drawing this boundary well requires some experience and taste.
Then we encode that limited set of operations into a type class. Here&rsquo;s one way
to do it.
</p>



<label class="input">In[1]:</label><div class="org-src-container">
<pre class="src src-haskell"><code>class CircuitOperations m where
 make_wire :: m Wire
 get_signal :: Wire -&gt; m Signal
 set_signal :: Wire -&gt; Signal -&gt; m ()
 add_action :: Wire -&gt; m () -&gt; m ()
 after_delay :: Int -&gt; m () -&gt; m ()
 get_time :: m Int
 probe_output :: String -&gt; m ()
 propagate :: m ()
</code></pre>
</div>



<p>
Having read the previous article, these operations should sound familiar. I&rsquo;m
sure it&rsquo;s possible to create a better set of operations, but this will suffice
to illustrate the point.
